{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; Airbus SAS Airplanes",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 09, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-09/pdf/2025-22351.pdf",
    "summary": "The Federal Aviation Administration has issued new and updated safety rules that require inspections and fixes on certain Airbus A300 airplanes and Guimbal Cabri G2 helicopters, with an effective date of January 13, 2026. The goal is to find and repair cracks in critical areas before they threaten the strength of the aircraft.\n\nFor the Airbus fleet, the agency is replacing a 2017 rule and expanding it to cover more models, while removing a few that no longer apply. Operators must inspect joints in the upper wing and, if needed, modify parts in that area to better resist cracking. If damage is found, repairs must be done before the airplane flies again. The change follows European findings that stress in this part of the wing can lead to cracks forming at the same time in nearby joints. The agency says the update won\u2019t increase overall costs compared to what was proposed, and it will accept previously approved alternative compliance methods to reduce paperwork. It estimates about 119 U.S. airplanes are affected, with typical inspection and modification costs running into the low five figures per airplane.\n\nFor the Guimbal Cabri G2 helicopter, reports of cracks in a key rotor control part have led to a new rule. Owners must repeatedly inspect that part and replace it if any cracks are found. Installing a part that hasn\u2019t passed inspection is not allowed.\n\nThese actions most directly benefit passengers, crews, and communities by reducing the chance of structural problems in flight. They also give airlines, cargo operators, and maintenance teams clear steps to keep older fleets safe and extend service life, while aligning U.S. requirements with European guidance to avoid duplication.\n\nOn the other hand, operators\u2014especially cargo carriers using aging A300s and smaller flight schools or private owners flying Cabri G2s\u2014may face extra maintenance time, scheduling disruptions, and added costs for inspections and replacements. While the agency does not expect a significant economic impact on small businesses, the burden will be felt most by those with tighter margins or limited spare aircraft.\n\nThe publication also includes technical references and access details for required service documents from manufacturers, along with contacts at the agency for further information.",
